{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target conflates corroboration of a stance with falsification of the audited content. Two NU verdicts (SRC_006, SRC_008) sharing a falsificationist disposition can sustain a ritual flag without any one of them demonstrating a factual defect in the target's reasoning. The audit standard (§11.3) asks whether adjudication-ritual flags are sustained; the target answers by explaining *why* they persist epistemically, but never establishes that the underlying criticism they encode actually holds against the audited claim. Sustaining a flag is not justified by neighborhood agreement alone — the flag must latch onto a real fault, and the target substitutes social epistemology for that factual link.","premise":"SRC_001's argument depends on treating independent corroboration of a falsificationist stance as equivalent to the existence of a concrete fault in the audited claim, but the two are not the same.","premise_evidence":null,"counterexample":["Two independent reviewers can both endorse a 'falsificationist' critique of a claim (agreeing that it is not falsifiable) while the claim is in fact readily falsifiable by an unstated observation; in that case the ritual flag is sustained by neighborhood consensus yet no genuine fault exists, showing that corroboration of a stance does not entail a factual defect."]}]}